Dear Laura,

Solidon3D 0.3.0 has been released. The desktop application adapts imported 3D
models, constructs printable parts from dimensions and tested components, and
prepares them for external slicing. Its non-destructive operation stack
bridges imported geometry, printable features and material data without moving
projects into a cloud platform.

Its newest demonstrable workflow changes the diameter of a recognised hole in
an imported STL or 3MF model while preserving the original location and axis,
including angled features. The modification stays as a parameterised
operation. Users can add standards-derived fasteners, inserts and bearing
seats, then drive their fit from a printed calibration artefact.

Material configuration is attached to each physical filament spool rather
than a generic colour slot. Temperatures, cooling, retraction and material
properties are written into the correct 3MF material position and mapped to
the external slicer handoff. Analysis and imported G-code metrics remain
explicitly separated by provenance.

Since the first public 0.1 demo, 220 curated user-facing changes have also
added constrained in-viewport sketching, exact-body operations and reusable
parametric parts stored as safe recipes of registered operations. An optional
LLM may call the same API as the UI, but it cannot calculate geometry or run
generated source.

Solidon runs locally on Windows, macOS and Linux, with no account, telemetry
or cloud project store. Overview: https://solidon3d.de/en/

I can send a compact technical briefing, the 0.3.0 build, sample STL/3MF pair,
